    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d392">

                <pb facs="0499" n="489" xml:id="pg489"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">693.0023/1–2947: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Dairen</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Benninghoff</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                        Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dairen</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-01-29T11:00:00+09:00">January 29,
                            1947—11 a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received January 31—6:56 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>19. Although visit of courier ship was without incident and superficially
                    cordial, Dept should know that only one officer was allowed ashore. Doctor
                    denied permission visit Vice Consul Patch’s ill daughter, sailors throwing
                    football on dock ordered back on ship. Would appreciate information treatment
                    Soviet ships U. S. controlled ports.</p>
                <p>Repeat Nanking, Shanghai, Moscow.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Benninghoff</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
